Why JVM-native agents matter# Most enterprise systems of record — banking cores, insurance platforms, ERP integrations — run on the JVM or .NET. Building agents in the same stack reuses existing security (Spring Security, OAuth), transactions, data access, messaging, observability and deployment pipelines, and keeps agents close to the services they call. Since 2025 the JVM has gained a real choice of agent frameworks. JVM agent stack flowchart TB subgraph Planning[&amp;#34;Agent frameworks&amp;#34;] EMB[&amp;#34;Embabel&amp;lt;br/&amp;gt;goal-oriented planning&amp;#34;] LCA[&amp;#34;LangChain4j agentic&amp;lt;br/&amp;gt;sequence, parallel, supervisor&amp;#34;] KOOG[&amp;#34;Koog&amp;lt;br/&amp;gt;Kotlin strategy graphs&amp;#34;] ADKJ[&amp;#34;ADK for Java&amp;#34;] end subgraph Primitives[&amp;#34;LLM primitives&amp;#34;] SAI[&amp;#34;Spring AI&amp;lt;br/&amp;gt;ChatClient, advisors, tools, MCP&amp;#34;] LC4J[&amp;#34;LangChain4j&amp;lt;br/&amp;gt;AI Services, tools, RAG&amp;#34;] end EMB --&amp;gt; SAI LCA --&amp;gt; LC4J KOOG --&amp;gt; P1[&amp;#34;Own model clients&amp;#34;] ADKJ --&amp;gt; P2[&amp;#34;Own model clients&amp;#34;] SAI --&amp;gt; GW[&amp;#34;AI gateway → models&amp;#34;] LC4J --&amp;gt; GW Spring AI: patterns on a solid core# Spring AI provides the primitives — ChatClient, advisors, @Tool, MCP client/server, chat memory, structured output, observability (see Spring AI fundamentals). Agentic behaviour is built from those primitives using the workflow patterns: the tool-calling loop (optionally inside ToolCallingAdvisor), routing, chaining, orchestrator–workers and evaluator–optimizer. Orchestrator–workers in plain Spring AI: record SubTask(String type, String description) {} record Plan(List&amp;lt;SubTask&amp;gt; tasks) {} @Service class ImpactAnalysis { private final ChatClient planner; // stronger model private final ChatClient worker; // cheaper model ImpactAnalysis(@Qualifier(&amp;#34;smartClient&amp;#34;) ChatClient planner, @Qualifier(&amp;#